from review import card,save,DATE
i='TCS-4061';_,c=card(i);refs=c['references'];refs[0]['locator']='Section 2.1: UGC; Section 2.4: chains and minimum support; Theorems 14 and 19; Open Question 21.'
refs += [dict(id='full',title='Computational Topology and the Unique Games Conjecture — full version',authors='Joshua A. Grochow; Jamie Tucker-Foltz',year=2018,url='https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.06800',pdf_url='https://arxiv.org/pdf/1803.06800v1',locator='Submitted 19 March 2018, PDF dated 20 March 2018; Section 2.4, full Theorem 19 and Open Question 21. No later arXiv revision listed.'),dict(id='later',title='Homology localization through the looking-glass of parameterized complexity theory',authors='Nello Blaser; Erlend Raa Vågset',year=2025,url='https://doi.org/10.1007/s41468-025-00212-0',locator='Published 29 May 2025; Section 4, Theorem 2 and Corollary 1: parameterized approximation hardness on general complexes; Section 6, Theorem 6: exact ETH lower bound on weighted surfaces. Coefficients are ℤ₂ throughout.')]
notes=['Recovered ∀ approximation constant c>1 ∃ coefficient modulus k=k(c), fixed independently of the instance.','Defined simplicial surface input, cycles modulo k, homologous representatives via boundaries of 2-chains, and unweighted edge-support minimization.','Kept homology and triangulations distinct from cohomology and general cell decompositions; these cannot be exchanged for free in the approximation statement.','Specified the source’s NP-hardness interpretation of UGC, exact approximation output and unbounded surface topology.','Read the full original question and checked the 2025 parameterized results, which concern different restrictions or exact computation.']
save(i,dict(title='UGC hardness of homology localization',formal='Does the Unique Games Conjecture imply that, for every fixed approximation factor c>1, there exists an integer k=k(c)≥2 such that c-approximating 1-Homology Localization over ℤ/kℤ on triangulated 2-manifolds is NP-hard? Given a finite triangulation K and a cycle z∈C₁(K;ℤ/kℤ), the task is to output a homologous cycle z′ of minimum possible support up to factor c: |supp(z′)|≤c·OPT(K,z), where OPT(K,z)=min_{u∈C₂(K;ℤ/kℤ)}|supp(z+∂₂u)|. The modulus may depend on c but is fixed before the input triangulation is supplied.',definitions='''A triangulation is a finite abstract simplicial complex of dimension 2, encoded by a list of vertices, edges and triangles with integer vertex labels and all faces present. Its realization is promised to be a compact 2-manifold without boundary; no bound on genus, number of vertices or number of components is fixed. Equivalently for this finite pure complex, the link of every vertex is a simplicial cycle. No orientability restriction is added. This uses the closed-surface setting of the source’s topological comparison; orientable surfaces are included. Cells with arbitrary polygonal attaching maps are not valid replacements for triangles, and edge weights are all one.

Fix an ordering of vertices and orient each simplex by increasing order. C_d(K;ℤ/kℤ) is the module of coefficient vectors indexed by d-simplices, with all arithmetic modulo k. There is no assumption that k is prime. The boundary of an oriented simplex is ∂[v₀,…,v_d]=∑_{j=0}^d (−1)^j[v₀,…,v̂ⱼ,…,v_d], extended linearly. The input z is an explicit residue vector on the edges with ∂₁z=0. Two such cycles are homologous if z′−z=∂₂u for some 2-chain u, meaning an arbitrary coefficient vector on triangles. The 2-chain need not itself be a cycle. Support is the set of edges with nonzero coefficients; its cost counts each such edge once, independently of the coefficient’s residue. This is the unweighted ℓ₀ support objective, not the sum of coefficient magnitudes, geometric length, minimum enclosing radius or the number of connected components. The minimum exists because there are finitely many 2-chains. A feasible approximation must return an explicit edge vector z′ in the same homology class. If OPT=0, its output must have zero support. No single-loop or connected-output requirement is imposed.

For fixed c and k, polynomial time refers to the binary length of the full triangulation and residue-vector description; the polynomial may depend on those fixed constants. NP-hardness of the approximation search task is understood in the usual oracle-reduction sense: a deterministic polynomial-time procedure can decide 3-SAT with access to an oracle returning any feasible c-approximation, and must work for every valid answer that the oracle could return. Here 3-SAT asks whether a conjunction of clauses with at most three Boolean literals is satisfiable. A promise-gap many-one reduction with separated objective values is in particular sufficient. This fixes the source’s NP-hard interpretation of “hard”; it does not assert a stronger randomized-time lower bound.

A Unique Games instance has a finite graph of variables, a label set [q]={1,…,q}, and for each edge uv a permutation π_uv of [q], explicitly encoded, with the constraint ℓ(v)=π_uv(ℓ(u)). Its value is the maximum fraction of edge constraints satisfied by a labeling. Parallel constraints may be listed separately. The UGC says that for all rational ε,δ>0 with ε+δ<1, some fixed q makes it NP-hard to distinguish value at least 1−ε from value at most δ; intermediate values are outside the promise. Thus the claim’s quantifiers are UGC ⇒ ∀c>1 ∃k≥2 NP-hard(c-approximation at modulus k). Restricting c to rational constants greater than one gives the same all-constant target by monotonicity of approximation guarantees. It does not demand a single modulus that works for all c, and it does not set k=2.''',question_type='yes_no',answer_criterion='A positive answer proves the stated conditional NP-hardness for every constant c>1 with a modulus depending only on c, producing valid triangulated surfaces and the specified minimum-support homology task. A negative answer must refute that conditional claim in this model. A result for only one constant factor, general cell decompositions, higher-dimensional manifolds, geometric localization or exact rather than approximate optimization does not settle the all-constant claim.',context_blocks=[dict(title='The representative must stay in the same class',text='The optimization changes a cycle by boundaries of 2-chains while preserving its homology class. It seeks a sparse edge-support representative, which may have multiple components and coefficients in a cyclic group larger than ℤ₂.',citation='primary'),dict(title='What the source already proves',text='Theorem 14 proves the strong Unique Games equivalence for general cell decompositions of closed orientable surfaces. Theorem 19 gives a 7/6−η inapproximability factor on triangulated surfaces under UGC, with a modulus depending on η. Open Question 21 asks for all constant factors in the triangulated setting.',citation='primary'),dict(title='Duality does not preserve every input restriction',text='The source relates homology and cohomology using dual cell decompositions. The dual of a triangulation need not itself be a triangulation. The card therefore retains the stated homology-on-triangulations task rather than treating a cohomology or arbitrary-cell result as automatically equivalent for this objective.',citation='full'),dict(title='Later parameterized results have different scope',text='Blaser and Vågset prove parameterized constant-factor hardness on general simplicial complexes and an exact ETH lower bound on a weighted surface problem over ℤ₂. Their 2025 statements do not provide the all-constant, unweighted triangulated-surface approximation result asked here.',citation='later')],why='Tests whether the strong approximation obstruction associated with Unique Games persists when topological inputs are restricted to genuine surface triangulations. It separates topological equivalence from approximation-preserving representation and connects discrete optimization with the sparsity of homology representatives.',progress=[dict(date='2018',text='The source proves the cell-decomposition result and a 7/6−η triangulation bound, then asks for all constant factors on triangulations.',citation='primary'),dict(date='2025-05-29',text='Parameterized homology-localization work gives different general-complex approximation and weighted-surface exact lower bounds.',citation='later')],references=refs,status='open',status_note='Read the source’s homology and approximation definitions, Theorems 14 and 19 and Open Question 21 in the conference and full versions. Checked the 2025 paper’s coefficient convention and Theorems 2 and 6. Searches through 12 September 2026 found no resolution of this exact all-constant triangulated-surface target; searches are not exhaustive and the cited proofs were not independently audited.',model_self_contained=True,requires_context=False,review_note='Restored the complete modulus quantifier and the unweighted ℓ₀ homology objective. Closed surfaces follow the paper’s setting; no extra orientability promise is imposed. “Hard” is instantiated by the NP-hard UGC convention the source states, rather than an unstated BPP or quasipolynomial-time convention. No solution strategy is included.',importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='A substantive link between Unique Games approximation hardness and the finite representations of surface homology, beyond a change in constants for arbitrary complexes. Existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),working_summary=dict(sentences=['Homology localization minimizes the number of edges supporting a cycle in a prescribed homology class.','The question asks for Unique-Games-based hardness at every constant approximation factor on surface triangulations.','The coefficient modulus may depend on the desired hardness factor but cannot grow with the input.','The source proves only a smaller constant gap for triangulations while establishing the stronger result for general cell decompositions.','Later parameterized results checked here do not settle the precise unweighted all-constant approximation target.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','full','later'])),notes=notes,checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs])
